    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>As a first time home buyer, Seattle can feel like a market designed for people who have already done this three times. It does not have to be. With a realistic budget, a solid pre-approval, and a clear understanding of how Seattle offers actually work, you can buy with confidence instead of guesswork. This guide walks through each step, from your first spreadsheet to the day you get your keys.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Build a Budget That Fits Seattle Life</h2>
<p>Start with the monthly payment you are comfortable with, not the maximum a lender will approve. Your housing cost is more than principal and interest. It also includes property taxes, homeowners insurance, possibly mortgage insurance, and HOA dues if you are looking at a condo or some townhomes.</p>
<p>Beyond the monthly number, set aside cash for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Down payment</strong>, which can range widely depending on your loan program.</li>
<li><strong>Closing costs</strong> such as escrow, title, lender fees, and prepaid items.</li>
<li><strong>Inspections</strong>, including a sewer scope, which is standard practice here.</li>
<li><strong>A move-in cushion</strong> for repairs, furniture, and the surprises every house has.</li>
</ul>
<p>One helpful reality check: Washington has no state income tax, which can leave a bit more room in your monthly budget than buyers coming from some other states expect. That said, everyday costs in Seattle are real, so build in breathing room.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Get Pre-Approved (Not Just Pre-Qualified)</h2>
<p>A pre-qualification is a quick estimate. A pre-approval means a lender has reviewed your income, assets, credit, and debts. In Seattle, sellers and their agents expect to see a pre-approval letter with every offer, and on competitive homes they may call your lender to confirm you are solid.</p>
<p>Talk to more than one lender. Compare loan types, rate options, fees, and how quickly they can close. A local lender who knows Seattle timelines and condo approval issues can be a real advantage. The <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau</a> has plain-language tools for comparing loan estimates side by side.</p>

<h2>First Time Home Buyer Seattle Programs Worth Knowing</h2>
<p>Many first-time buyers do not realize how much help may be available. Two main sources to explore:</p>
<h3>Washington State Housing Finance Commission</h3>
<p>The <a href="https://www.wshfc.org/">Washington State Housing Finance Commission</a> offers home loan programs and down payment assistance for eligible buyers, delivered through participating lenders. Programs typically have income and purchase price limits, and buyers are usually asked to complete a homebuyer education course. The class is worth taking even if you end up not using a program.</p>
<h3>City of Seattle Down Payment Assistance</h3>
<p>The <a href="https://www.seattle.gov/housing">City of Seattle Office of Housing</a> supports down payment assistance for income-qualified buyers purchasing within city limits, often through partner organizations. These programs can have specific property and occupancy rules, and funding availability changes, so check current guidelines before you shop.</p>
<p>We cannot tell you which program you qualify for, but a lender who works with these programs can. Ask early, because some programs add steps or time to your closing.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Shop Smart and Narrow Your Neighborhoods</h2>
<p>Seattle neighborhoods vary a lot in housing style, commute, and price. A first-time buyer might find an older condo on Capitol Hill, a townhome in Ballard, or a starter house farther north in <a href="/neighborhoods/lake-city/">Lake City</a> or south in <a href="/neighborhoods/columbia-city/">Columbia City</a>. Browse our <a href="/neighborhoods/">neighborhood guides</a> to compare areas, then set up a search on our <a href="/homes-for-sale/">current listings</a>.</p>
<p>Tour in person whenever you can, and visit on a rainy day if possible. Seattle homes look different in November than in July, and you want to see drainage, light, and street parking in real conditions.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Understand Seattle Offers</h2>
<p>Two features of the Seattle market surprise many newcomers.</p>
<h3>Offer Review Dates</h3>
<p>Many listings come on the market with an offer review date, usually several days after listing. The seller reviews all offers together on that date. This gives you time to tour, line up financing, and decide whether to do a pre-inspection. Sometimes a buyer submits a "preemptive" offer before the review date, which the seller may or may not accept.</p>
<h3>Escalation Clauses</h3>
<p>An escalation clause says you will beat any competing offer by a set amount, up to a maximum price you choose. It can keep you from overpaying while still staying competitive. The catch is that you must be truly comfortable at your cap, and you should think about how an appraisal below that number would affect you. We walk every client through these numbers before they sign anything.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Inspections and Due Diligence</h2>
<p>A general home inspection plus a side sewer scope is the typical starting point in Seattle. Depending on the home's age, your inspector may also flag roof moss, drainage and crawlspace moisture, old heating oil tanks, outdated wiring, or seismic issues. On competitive homes, buyers sometimes do a pre-inspection before the review date. On slower listings, a standard inspection contingency is often very doable.</p>
<p>If you are buying a condo, due diligence also means reviewing the association's budget, reserves, meeting minutes, and any planned special assessments.</p>

<h2>Step 6: From Mutual Acceptance to Closing</h2>
<p>Once your offer is accepted, escrow opens and the clock starts. Typical steps include:</p>
<ol>
<li>Depositing your earnest money with escrow.</li>
<li>Completing inspections within your contingency period, if you have one.</li>
<li>Your lender ordering the appraisal and finalizing underwriting.</li>
<li>Reviewing the title report and your Closing Disclosure.</li>
<li>Signing documents with escrow, usually a few days before the closing date.</li>
<li>Recording, funding, and getting your keys on the date set in your contract.</li>
</ol>
<p>Avoid opening new credit, changing jobs, or making big purchases during this window. Even small changes can delay underwriting.</p>

<h2>A Few Final Tips</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep your documents organized. Lenders will ask for updated pay stubs and statements.</li>
<li>Ask questions early. There are no silly questions on your first purchase.</li>
<li>Talk with a tax professional about how owning a home may affect your situation.</li>
</ul>

<p>Buying your first home here is a big step, and you do not have to figure it out alone. Our team helps first-time buyers every step of the way, from the first lender conversation to closing day. Visit our <a href="/buy-a-home-in-seattle/">Seattle home buying page</a> to learn how we work, or reach out and let's talk about your plans.</p>
